  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "Let $V=C[-1,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\u27e8f(t),g(t)\u27e9={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=t$ and $g(t)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\u27e8f(t),g(t)\u27e9=0$\n- $\u27e8f(t),g(t)\u27e9=\\pi $\n- $f(t)\u22a5g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $\u27e8\\cdot ,\\cdot \u27e9$ in $V$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n\u27e8f(t),g(t)\u27e9={\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & \u27f9 & {u}^{\u2032}=1, \\\\ {v}^{\u2032}=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t & \u27f9 & v=\\int {v}^{\u2032}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{cos}\u2061t\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{\u2032}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t){|}_{-1}^{1}-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(1)-(-1)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(-1))-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061t{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(1)+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(-1))+(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(1)-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(-1)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(1)-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(1))+(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(1)-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(1)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the inner product is zero, our vectors must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
